<BaseLayout title="Affiliate Disclosure" description="How HomeSpaGuide earns money, and how that shapes (and doesn't shape) our editorial.">
  <div class="container-narrow" style="padding-top: 2rem; padding-bottom: 4rem;">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Affiliate Disclosure' }]} />
    <article class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
      <h1>Affiliate Disclosure</h1>

      <p><strong>HomeSpaGuide is a participant in the Amazon Services LLC Associates Program</strong>, an affiliate advertising program designed to provide a means for sites to earn advertising fees by advertising and linking to Amazon.com.</p>

      <p>What that means in plain English: when you click a "View on Amazon" link on our site and buy something — that product or any other — Amazon pays us a small commission. The price you pay is exactly the same as if you had gone to Amazon.com directly.</p>

      <h2>How it affects our editorial</h2>
      <p>It doesn’t. Editor Picks and our scoring rubric are independent of commission rates, and we recommend inexpensive products whenever they are the right choice. See our <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2>Amazon trademark</h2>
      <p>Amazon and the Amazon logo are trademarks of Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates.</p>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>Some pages may display ads served by Google AdSense. Ads are always labelled “Advertisement” and advertisers have no influence on our editorial content. See our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for how ad cookies work.</p>

      <h2>Other programs</h2>
      <p>Beyond Amazon Associates and display advertising, we do not run sponsored posts, paid placements, or undisclosed product partnerships. If that ever changes, we'll disclose it here and on the affected pages.</p>

      <h2>Questions?</h2>
      <p>Reach us via <a href="/contact">our contact form</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
